\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Quaternion units and the complex unit}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The units #{i}, #{j} and #{k} in \ref{fdip-00A0} are the
quaternion units of #{\mathbb H}. Mathematical physics writes
those units #{i,j,k}, and writes #{\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H}
for their real span. Geometric calculus has no separate
quaternion unit: a square root of #{(-1)} there is a unit
bivector or the pseudoscalar. The quaternion #{i} is not the
complex unit #{\ui}. The right complex structure in the
proposition is the real span of #{1} and that quaternion
#{i}.}
